Game Prompt v3 — Phrase Builder: Tonal Match

Overview
This game is a 2–4 player musical phrase card game inspired by Apples to Apples and Uno. Players build short musical phrases from beat-sized tokens, using a shared flop of random cards and a rotating judge. The judge chooses the best phrase for the round target, which is based on a tonal family and/or a chordal goal. The game rewards musicality, coherence, and phrase-building skill more than technical theory.

Core Design Principle
The game is intentionally built as a social judgment game, not a strict theory exam.

- Objective constraints keep phrases legal.
- Human judgment decides which phrase is strongest.
- The card pool creates tactical decision-making.
- The public target creates a shared frame of reference, like Apples to Apples.

This is a phrase-building game first and a music theory exercise second.

1. Core token logic
1. Each token represents one beat.
2. One beat equals one quarter note.
3. A token may contain no more than four subdivisions of the beat.
   - Maximum: 4 sixteenth notes
4. The playable pitch range is C3 to G4.
5. Every note in a token must be either:
   - a chord tone of the active tonal family, or
   - a known non-chord tone accepted under Baroque convention
    (for example: escape tones, leading tones, appoggiaturas, suspensions, passing tones, neighboring tones, accented lower neighbors, etc.)
6. The game supports four tonal families:
   - C major
   - F major
   - G major
   - D minor
7. Token values and card types:
   - 0 = Quarter Rest
   - 1–3 = Quarter Note
   - 4–6 = Pair of Eighth Notes
   - 7–8 = Four Sixteenth Notes
   - 9 = 3:2 Tuplet (three eighth-note triplets in one beat, in the time of two eighth notes)
8. Duplicate tokens are required to make the deck statistically useful.
   - Start with 4 copies of each token.

2. Game structure
- 2–4 players
- 1 active judge each round
- 1 shared flop of 4 random cards
- 1 deck built from the token set
- Each player holds a hand of 6 cards at the start of a round

3. Objective
Players build phrases from their hand and the shared flop. Each phrase must be exactly 8 beats long and must be playable as a short melodic idea or musical sentence.

The goal is to create the most convincing phrase for the round target.

4. Shared flop
The flop is a shared pool of random cards drawn from the deck.

Rules:
- The flop contains 4 cards.
- Players may use 2–4 flop cards in a phrase; with a 6-card hand, at least 2 shared cards are required to reach 8 beats.
- The flop is refreshed each round.
- The physical flop is random. During balance testing, the simulator uses a progression-weighted flop so every player can produce a legal phrase while deck ratios are being tuned.

5. Round target
A round target is a tonal progression, optionally framed by a tonal family.

Default round target format:
- Target progression: G->C perfect authentic cadence
- Target progression: F->C imperfect cadence
- Target progression: Dm->G->C ii-V-I cadence
- Implied tonal family is usually C major when the phrase resolves to the tonic.

Examples:
- Best phrase that resolves through G->C with a clear perfect cadence
- Best phrase that leads through F->C as an imperfect cadence
- Best phrase that realizes Dm->G->C as a convincing 2-5-1 arrival

Round target rules:
- The target is announced before players build their phrase.
- The judge chooses the target or the table agrees on one.
- The judge should prefer a target that is simple and playable for a short round.
- Players must consider the intended cadence and end of phrase as part of the phrase design, not just the local family color.
- The 8 beat positions have explicit tonal-family zones:
  - G->C: beats 1–4 use G-major tokens; beats 5–8 use C-major tokens.
  - F->C: beats 1–4 use F-major tokens; beats 5–8 use C-major tokens.
  - Dm->G->C: beats 1–2 use D-minor tokens; beats 3–4 use G-major tokens; beats 5–8 use C-major tokens.
- Cards selected from the hand and flop may be played in any order, but each card must fit the tonal family required at its chosen beat.

6. Hand and discard
Players begin each round with a hand of 6 cards.

Rules:
- Players may draw from the deck to replenish their hand after a discard.
- Each player gets one discard action per round.
- A discard must be followed by a draw from the deck.
- Players may not discard after the phrase has been finalized.

This keeps the game tactical without slowing the round too much.

7. Phrase construction rules
A legal phrase must satisfy all of the following:
- Length is exactly 8 beats total.
- It is made only from legal token units.
- Tokens may not be rearranged internally in a way that breaks the token definition.
- Whole tokens from the hand and flop may be arranged in any order.
- Notes must fit within C3 to G4.
- Notes must be legal in the active tonal family or be acceptable as a recognized Baroque non-chord tone.
- The final phrase must support the target progression and hear as a convincing cadence or harmonic arrival.
- The phrase must be playable and singable without impossible fingerings or unsupported leaps.
- The phrase should feel like a musical sentence, not just a random collection of notes.

Important rule:
A phrase does not have to be “strictly academic” to be legal. It must be musical, coherent, and defensible within the tonal target.

8. Performance and reveal
Once players have finished building a phrase, each player presents it.

Rules:
- Players may play it on an instrument or sing it.
- Players reveal their phrase in a turn order determined by the judge or by table agreement.
- The judge does not vote.
- The judge listens to each phrase and compares how well it matches the round target.

9. Judging
The game is judged by human vote only.

Rules:
- All non-judging players vote on which phrase is best.
- The judge does not vote.
- The judge chooses the winner based on which phrase best fits the tonal target and feels most musical.
- Voting is based on musicality and round fit, not on strict notation or theory exam scoring.

This is the core Apples-to-Apples dynamic: there is no single mathematically correct phrase, only a strongest interpretation of the prompt.

10. Scoring
Suggested score model:
- Winner of the round: 2 points
- Runner-up: 1 point
- Optional bonus: +1 if the phrase resolves clearly to the tonic or to a strong cadence in the target family
- Optional bonus: +1 if the phrase uses the flop in a particularly musical way

The game should not become a theory puzzle. The score should reward musical clarity, coherence, and phrase quality.

11. Winner and end of game
The player with the most points at the end of a fixed number of rounds wins.

Suggested game length:
- 6 to 12 rounds
- Or until one player reaches a target score, such as 10 points

12. Role of the judge
The judge may rotate each round, but the table may also choose a more confident sight reader when needed.

Recommended default:
- Judge rotates every round
- If the table is inexperienced, the most confident sight reader may act as judge for a round

13. Wild cards and expansions
Wild cards are intentionally excluded from the base game.

This keeps the system clean and the decision space manageable.

Future expansion possibilities:
- wild cards
- bonus phrase objectives
- more tonal families
- special rule cards
- deck asymmetry by difficulty

14. Why this works as a game
This design works because it combines:
- a clear public target
- a short phrase-building problem
- a noisy but bounded card system
- a social voting model
- a human judge evaluating musicality rather than objective correctness

This is the same reason Apples to Apples works so well: it gives every player a shared target, many plausible answers, and a fast, social decision process.

15. Final implementation intent
This game is intended to be:
- musical enough to feel expressive
- simple enough to be fun and social
- structured enough to support digital play and automated validation later

The base game is a short-form, tonal phrase-building card game with social voting. The model should support phrase legality checks and tonal-family validation in future development, but the round should remain judged by humans first.

Final rule summary
- 2–4 players
- 6-card starting hand
- 1 discard per round
- 4-card random flop
- Round target is a harmonic progression and implied tonal family
- 8-beat phrase
- Human vote decides winner
- Judge rotates by default
- Musicality wins over absolute theory

This is the working game rule for the current design iteration.
